// Eviction Management System - Cursor Rules

// Project Structure
- Flask application for eviction management
- CSV import functionality for eviction data
- Eviction timeline calculator
- Reporting system
- Payment processing with Stripe
- User authentication

// Important Files
- app.py: Main application file with routes and configuration
- simplified_app.py: Simplified version with core functionality
- eviction_timeline_calculator.py: Timeline calculation logic
- csv_import_tools.py: CSV import utilities

// Database Structure
- User data (landlords, tenants, administrators)
- Property records
- Eviction cases
- Reports
- Payment history

// Note: Sensitive Information
- Stripe API keys are stored in environment variables
- No actual sensitive user data should be committed to the repository
- Use .env files for local development

// Coding Conventions
- Flask routes are organized by functionality
- Templates extend from base.html
- Bootstrap is used for styling
- RESTful API conventions for endpoints

// Common Libraries
- Flask for web framework
- Pandas for data manipulation
- Stripe for payment processing
- SQLAlchemy for database ORM
- Jinja2 for templating

// Security Considerations
- Never commit API keys or secrets
- Use environment variables for sensitive information
- Sanitize user inputs
- Implement proper authentication and authorization

// Development Workflow
- Use virtual environment for dependencies
- Run with Flask development server during development
- Database migrations handled with Flask-Migrate
- Test payments with Stripe test mode

// Deployment
- Configure production settings before deployment
- Use proper web server (Gunicorn, uWSGI) in production
- Set DEBUG=False in production
- Use secure HTTPS connections

# Project Structure
- The 'rules' directory contains categorized .cursorrules examples
- Each .cursorrules file is focused on a specific technology or framework
- The README.md contains the full catalog of available rules

# Coding Conventions
- Use consistent naming conventions
- Include helpful comments for complex logic

# Common Libraries and Tools
- List frequently used libraries and dependencies here
- Include preferred versions when applicable

# Style Guidelines
- Maintain consistent formatting
- Follow established patterns in existing code
- Use appropriate error handling

